1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,560
July fourteenth, fifteen eighteen.

2
00:00:02,780 --> 00:00:03,674
Strasbourg.

3
00:00:03,894 --> 00:00:07,116
A woman steps into a narrow street, and starts to dance.

4
00:00:07,616 --> 00:00:08,855
There is no music.

5
00:00:09,075 --> 00:00:10,267
There is no party.

6
00:00:10,487 --> 00:00:11,679
There is no reason.

7
00:00:12,179 --> 00:00:13,898
She does not stop for days.

8
00:00:14,118 --> 00:00:16,116
And then her neighbours start dancing too.

9
00:00:16,616 --> 00:00:20,896
If you are still recovering from a Halloween party last night, relax.

10
00:00:21,116 --> 00:00:23,103
Whatever you did, it was not this.

11
00:00:24,503 --> 00:00:31,220
Hi, I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and today's file is the strangest dance floor in history.

12
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:33,597
The Dancing Plague of fifteen eighteen.

13
00:00:34,097 --> 00:00:35,286
Hundreds of people.

14
00:00:35,506 --> 00:00:37,637
A whole city council trying to fix it.

15
00:00:37,857 --> 00:00:40,856
And a cure so bad it might have made everything worse.

16
00:00:41,356 --> 00:00:48,437
So let's read the chronicles, see what actually happened, and find out why a city full of people could not stop moving.

17
00:00:49,837 --> 00:00:54,267
First, you need to know what life in Strasbourg felt like right before this.

18
00:00:54,767 --> 00:00:56,192
The harvests had failed.

19
00:00:56,412 --> 00:00:57,801
Bread prices shot up.

20
00:00:58,021 --> 00:00:59,738
Hail storms wrecked the crops.

21
00:00:59,958 --> 00:01:01,647
People were genuinely starving.

22
00:01:02,147 --> 00:01:03,949
On top of that came disease.

23
00:01:04,169 --> 00:01:08,537
Smallpox, leprosy, and a terrifying new illness called syphilis.

24
00:01:09,037 --> 00:01:14,663
If the early fifteen hundreds had a group chat, every message would just be the skull emoji.

25
00:01:15,163 --> 00:01:16,320
Keep that in mind.

26
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:18,780
Because it is going to matter a lot later.

27
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:22,687
Most chronicles give our first dancer a name.

28
00:01:22,907 --> 00:01:23,951
Frau Troffea.

29
00:01:24,171 --> 00:01:25,287
Mrs Troffea.

30
00:01:25,787 --> 00:01:30,553
She danced in the street outside her home, alone, while her husband begged her to stop.

31
00:01:31,053 --> 00:01:32,618
She kept going for days.

32
00:01:32,838 --> 00:01:35,157
Four to six, according to one account.

33
00:01:35,377 --> 00:01:39,127
Some say a full week, only stopping to collapse and sleep.

34
00:01:39,627 --> 00:01:48,544
Eventually the authorities put her on a wagon and sent her about thirty miles away, to a shrine of Saint Vitus in a town called Saverne.

35
00:01:49,044 --> 00:01:50,705
The original patient zero.

36
00:01:50,925 --> 00:01:52,842
And no, not the Taylor Swift song.

37
00:01:54,242 --> 00:01:56,654
But Frau Troffea leaving did not end it.

38
00:01:56,874 --> 00:02:00,509
Within days, more than thirty people were dancing in the streets.

39
00:02:01,009 --> 00:02:04,645
By August, one family chronicle says around four hundred.

40
00:02:05,145 --> 00:02:07,323
Historians argue about the numbers.

41
00:02:07,543 --> 00:02:10,224
Estimates run from about fifty to four hundred.

42
00:02:10,444 --> 00:02:12,896
But everyone agrees on the basic picture.

43
00:02:13,396 --> 00:02:16,204
These people did not look like they were having fun.

44
00:02:16,424 --> 00:02:21,457
Witnesses described blank faces, thrashing arms, swollen and bleeding feet.

45
00:02:21,677 --> 00:02:25,251
They danced until they dropped, and then got up and danced again.

46
00:02:25,751 --> 00:02:27,172
This was not a party.

47
00:02:27,392 --> 00:02:29,063
And the people of Strasbourg knew it.

48
00:02:29,283 --> 00:02:31,193
Which brings us to the city council.

49
00:02:32,593 --> 00:02:34,323
The council asked the doctors.

50
00:02:34,543 --> 00:02:36,571
And the doctors had a diagnosis.

51
00:02:36,791 --> 00:02:37,633
Hot blood.

52
00:02:38,133 --> 00:02:42,846
Medical theory at the time said overheated blood could cause madness.

53
00:02:43,066 --> 00:02:45,045
The cure was not bleeding the patient.

54
00:02:45,265 --> 00:02:47,124
The cure was letting them sweat it out.

55
00:02:47,624 --> 00:02:52,381
In other words, the medical advice for people who could not stop dancing was.

56
00:02:52,601 --> 00:02:53,548
More dancing.

57
00:02:54,348 --> 00:02:56,361
I want you to sit with that for a second.

58
00:02:57,761 --> 00:02:59,274
And the council went all in.

59
00:02:59,774 --> 00:03:03,948
The carpenters' and tanners' guild halls were turned into dance halls.

60
00:03:04,168 --> 00:03:08,489
A wooden stage went up in the horse market, and another in the grain market.

61
00:03:08,989 --> 00:03:10,323
They hired musicians.

62
00:03:10,543 --> 00:03:11,608
Pipes and drums.

63
00:03:11,828 --> 00:03:17,941
They even paid strong, healthy people to dance alongside the sick and hold them up when they got tired.

64
00:03:18,441 --> 00:03:22,696
The city basically invented the world's first viral dance challenge.

65
00:03:22,916 --> 00:03:24,174
With government funding.

66
00:03:24,674 --> 00:03:25,775
It did not work.

67
00:03:25,995 --> 00:03:27,397
The music drew crowds.

68
00:03:27,617 --> 00:03:29,353
The crowds drew more dancers.

69
00:03:29,573 --> 00:03:31,645
And the dancing spread faster than ever.

70
00:03:33,045 --> 00:03:35,389
Now here's a question you should be asking.

71
00:03:35,609 --> 00:03:38,620
Why would watching people dance make you start dancing?

72
00:03:39,120 --> 00:03:40,467
The answer has a name.

73
00:03:40,687 --> 00:03:41,554
Saint Vitus.

74
00:03:42,054 --> 00:03:47,546
People in this region believed that Saint Vitus could punish sinners by forcing them to dance.

75
00:03:47,766 --> 00:03:49,022
A curse, basically.

76
00:03:49,242 --> 00:03:56,407
And there had already been more than ten dancing outbreaks along the Rhine and Moselle rivers since thirteen seventy four.

77
00:03:56,907 --> 00:04:02,256
So if you are starving, terrified, and you believe a saint might curse you into dancing.

78
00:04:02,476 --> 00:04:04,257
And then you see your neighbour dancing.

79
00:04:04,477 --> 00:04:05,565
Uncontrollably.

80
00:04:05,785 --> 00:04:06,617
In public.

81
00:04:07,117 --> 00:04:09,224
Your brain does not think, interesting.

82
00:04:09,444 --> 00:04:11,647
Your brain thinks, oh no, it's my turn.

83
00:04:12,147 --> 00:04:14,927
And that is not even the strangest part of this story.

84
00:04:16,327 --> 00:04:20,218
When the stages backfired, the council did a complete U turn.

85
00:04:20,718 --> 00:04:22,270
They tore the stages down.

86
00:04:22,490 --> 00:04:24,032
They banned public dancing.

87
00:04:24,252 --> 00:04:27,538
They banned almost all music until the end of September.

88
00:04:28,038 --> 00:04:29,294
There was one exception.

89
00:04:29,514 --> 00:04:33,217
Weddings and religious celebrations could still have music.

90
00:04:33,437 --> 00:04:36,619
But only quietly, at home, with stringed instruments.

91
00:04:36,839 --> 00:04:37,730
No drums.

92
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:39,130
No tambourines.

93
00:04:39,630 --> 00:04:40,797
Fifteen eighteen.

94
00:04:41,017 --> 00:04:45,185
The only year in history where the drummer got cancelled by the city.

95
00:04:46,585 --> 00:04:48,385
So they tried the other approach.

96
00:04:48,605 --> 00:04:49,326
Religion.

97
00:04:49,826 --> 00:04:57,449
The worst cases were loaded onto wagons and taken to the shrine of Saint Vitus at Saverne, a trip of about three days.

98
00:04:57,949 --> 00:05:05,236
There, priests placed them under a wooden statue of the saint, gave them small crosses, and put red shoes on their feet.

99
00:05:05,456 --> 00:05:09,456
Shoes sprinkled with holy water and marked with crosses of oil.

100
00:05:09,956 --> 00:05:11,036
And here's the thing.

101
00:05:11,256 --> 00:05:12,580
It seems to have worked.

102
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:15,012
Within a few weeks, the dancing faded.

103
00:05:15,232 --> 00:05:17,147
By early September, it was over.

104
00:05:17,647 --> 00:05:22,142
Which, if it really was about fear of Saint Vitus, makes a lot of sense.

105
00:05:22,362 --> 00:05:23,511
The fear started it.

106
00:05:23,731 --> 00:05:25,044
The forgiveness ended it.

107
00:05:26,444 --> 00:05:26,973
Now.

108
00:05:27,193 --> 00:05:28,810
The darkest claim in this story.

109
00:05:29,030 --> 00:05:31,057
That people danced themselves to death.

110
00:05:31,557 --> 00:05:36,433
Some later accounts say that at the peak, up to fifteen people died every day.

111
00:05:36,653 --> 00:05:39,271
That number gets repeated everywhere online.

112
00:05:39,771 --> 00:05:44,292
But the city's own records from the time do not mention any deaths at all.

113
00:05:44,512 --> 00:05:47,148
The big numbers come from chronicles written later.

114
00:05:47,648 --> 00:05:51,711
So it's possible some people died of exhaustion or heart failure.

115
00:05:51,931 --> 00:05:53,566
But nobody knows how many.

116
00:05:53,786 --> 00:05:56,287
And the famous figure may be exaggerated.

117
00:05:57,687 --> 00:06:05,203
About eight years later, a famous and famously arrogant doctor named Paracelsus visited Strasbourg and wrote about it.

118
00:06:05,703 --> 00:06:07,854
His diagnosis of Frau Troffea?

119
00:06:08,074 --> 00:06:09,151
She was not sick.

120
00:06:09,371 --> 00:06:11,087
She was doing it on purpose.

121
00:06:11,307 --> 00:06:12,609
To annoy her husband.

122
00:06:13,109 --> 00:06:15,374
And the other women, he said, copied her.

123
00:06:15,594 --> 00:06:17,433
Also to annoy their husbands.

124
00:06:18,233 --> 00:06:20,487
Paracelsus, ladies and gentlemen.

125
00:06:20,707 --> 00:06:21,757
Brilliant chemist.

126
00:06:21,977 --> 00:06:23,508
Terrible marriage counsellor.

127
00:06:24,908 --> 00:06:26,267
So what really happened?

128
00:06:26,487 --> 00:06:27,548
Modern theories.

129
00:06:27,768 --> 00:06:28,650
Theory one.

130
00:06:28,870 --> 00:06:29,926
Ergot poisoning.

131
00:06:30,426 --> 00:06:33,095
Ergot is a fungus that grows on damp rye.

132
00:06:33,315 --> 00:06:39,319
It contains chemicals related to LSD, and it can cause hallucinations and convulsions.

133
00:06:39,819 --> 00:06:40,865
Sounds perfect.

134
00:06:41,085 --> 00:06:42,039
One problem.

135
00:06:42,259 --> 00:06:45,577
Ergot also cuts off blood flow to your arms and legs.

136
00:06:45,797 --> 00:06:50,203
People poisoned with it could barely walk, never mind dance for days.

137
00:06:50,703 --> 00:06:51,624
Theory two.

138
00:06:51,844 --> 00:06:56,596
The one most historians favour today, argued by historian John Waller.

139
00:06:57,096 --> 00:06:58,738
Mass psychogenic illness.

140
00:06:58,958 --> 00:07:06,100
Extreme stress, hunger and fear, plus a shared belief in a dancing curse, pushed people into a kind of trance.

141
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:07,512
And trances spread.

142
00:07:08,012 --> 00:07:11,655
It sounds strange, but it has happened in modern times too.

143
00:07:11,875 --> 00:07:20,354
In nineteen sixty two, a laughing epidemic in Tanganyika, now Tanzania, spread through schools and villages for months.

144
00:07:21,754 --> 00:07:23,479
So here's the honest version.

145
00:07:23,979 --> 00:07:24,917
Documented.

146
00:07:25,137 --> 00:07:31,665
In the summer of fifteen eighteen, a large number of people in Strasbourg danced uncontrollably for weeks.

147
00:07:31,885 --> 00:07:36,187
The council built stages and hired musicians, then banned music.

148
00:07:36,407 --> 00:07:39,023
Dancers were sent to the shrine of Saint Vitus.

149
00:07:39,243 --> 00:07:40,674
By September, it ended.

150
00:07:41,174 --> 00:07:41,904
Legend.

151
00:07:42,124 --> 00:07:43,651
That it was definitely ergot.

152
00:07:43,871 --> 00:07:46,226
That exactly fifteen people a day died.

153
00:07:46,446 --> 00:07:50,632
And that Frau Troffea was just trying to win an argument with her husband.

154
00:07:51,132 --> 00:07:54,226
What it really shows is how fear can move a whole crowd.

155
00:07:54,446 --> 00:07:55,171
Literally.

156
00:07:55,671 --> 00:07:56,613
And honestly?

157
00:07:56,833 --> 00:08:03,293
A town that's starving, sick and scared, dancing because the world feels completely out of control.

158
00:08:03,513 --> 00:08:04,948
Scroll your feed today.

159
00:08:05,168 --> 00:08:07,195
That trend never really went away.

160
00:08:08,595 --> 00:08:09,707
So, your verdict.

161
00:08:09,927 --> 00:08:15,102
Poison, panic, or Paracelsus was right and it was all a marriage argument?

162
00:08:15,602 --> 00:08:16,847
Tell me in the comments.

163
00:08:17,067 --> 00:08:18,303
I'm going with panic.

164
00:08:18,523 --> 00:08:22,151
Mostly because I'd never survive a three day dance off.

165
00:08:22,651 --> 00:08:26,729
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

166
00:08:26,949 --> 00:08:31,332
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
